{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>A land which HaShem your Elohim seeks continually.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): the principal object of His providence is that land, for certainly He seeks all lands, but the intent is that the principal seeking and providence is there, and from there it spreads to the rest of the lands — like the heart in man, which is placed in the middle of the body, being the principal source of vitality, and from there the vitality spreads to the rest of the organs. And Scripture comes to teach us that the land of Israel is not placed under the governance of the stars and constellations like the other lands, nor is it handed over to the oversight of the supernal angels (אלהי מעלה) like the other climes, but the Holy One, blessed be He, in His own Person and glory seeks it continually; He did not appoint over it any officer or ruler from the other forces. And this is what David, peace be upon him, said concerning the land of Israel (Psalms 87:7): ״כל מעיני בך״ — 'all my springs are in You' — explaining that the principal focus of my providence and attention is upon You, for from there it spreads to the rest of the world." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: ״ארץ אשר ה׳ אלהיך דורש אותה תמיד״ — this is the Temple (ביהמ״ק) on high, concerning which Scripture said (Isaiah 66:1): ״והארץ הדום רגלי״ — 'and the earth is My footstool.' For 'the feet of man' is its ending, and therefore He mentioned in it ״ה׳ אלהיך״, and from this the Temple below is called (ibid., Isaiah 64:9) ״בית קדשנו ותפארתנו״ — 'the house of our holiness and our glory' — that is, the house of our Holy One and our Glory, for 'our Holy One' is the Glory, and this is the secret of the verse (Psalms 96:6): ״עוז ותפארת במקדשו״." "<b>And you should</b> apply your mind to what was mentioned in the verse above: ״והארץ אשר אתם עברים שמה״ — 'and the land that you are passing over to inherit it' — that is the physical land in which the Temple below is situated, and this is why it mentioned in it mountains and hills, ״למטר השמים תשתה מים״ — 'it drinks water from the rain of heaven.' Understand that that land drinks water from the rain of heaven, but this one — ״בשמים״ — is in heaven, and it is the tenth (העשירית), and this is the meaning of the word תמי״ד, namely: the measure (מדת) of יו״ד, and from this that land was praised with ten things (Deuteronomy 8:8): ״ארץ חטה ושעורה וגפן ותאנה ורמון ארץ זית שמן ודבש״ — which are the seven species, and fox-ear barley, rye (שיפון), and spelt (כוסמין) — behold, ten — and all of it is an allusion to the Land mentioned in the section of Genesis." "<b>From the beginning of the year.</b> It is written defective (חסר), without an אל״ף, and the allusion is to the month of Tishrei, which is the New Year for the creation of the world. And our Rabbis, of blessed memory, interpreted (מראש השנה): from the New Year it is judged what will be at its end."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {0 "The phrase אלהי מעלה is rendered 'supernal angels' following the medieval philosophical idiom for celestial intelligences; it could also read 'supernal powers.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:47:37.584297214Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}